@startuml
title .NET — stable ILogger integration and original timestamp preservation
skinparam backgroundColor white
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName SansSerif
skinparam ArrowColor #425466
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
hide footbox
autonumber

actor Application as app
participant DefaultLifecycle as core #EDF5FF
participant OpenTelemetryLifecycleSink as sink #EAF7EE
participant "ILogger / OTel bridge" as bridge #FFF1D6
participant LifecycleLogRecordProcessor as timestamp #EAF7EE
participant "SDK processors /\nexporter" as exporter #FFF1D6

note over bridge, exporter
  Host registers AddLifecycleTelemetry() BEFORE exporters.
  Enable Information-level logging for category Lifecycle.OpenTelemetry.
end note
app -> core : Phase(...), Milestone(...) or Condition(...)
core -> core : Validate and copy inputs\nAtomically update phase/condition, if applicable\nCapture wall time + monotonic elapsed\nCreate immutable LifecycleEvent
core -> sink : Emit(event)
sink -> sink : Copy typed attributes\nAdd lifecycle.internal.timestamp_ticks = event.Timestamp.UtcTicks
sink -> bridge : Log(Information, EventId(0, event.Name), attributes, ...)\nBody = event.Name
break Filtered out by host logging configuration
  bridge --> sink : No record emitted
  sink --> core
  core --> app
end
bridge -> bridge : Create LogRecord\nInitial Timestamp and ObservedTimestamp = SDK wall time\nCapture active Activity context
bridge -> timestamp : OnEnd(record), synchronously
timestamp -> timestamp : Match library category and timestamp marker\nSet Timestamp from original event ticks\nRemove private marker from attributes
timestamp --> bridge
bridge -> exporter : Continue to next configured processor\nTimestamp restored; ObservedTimestamp unchanged
note over bridge, exporter
  Host Resource supplies identity.
  OTLP exporter maps EventId.Name to native event_name.
  Batching, if configured, starts after timestamp restoration.
end note
exporter --> bridge
bridge --> sink
sink --> core
core --> app : Return

note over app, sink
  Invalid inputs fail before state changes. Ordinary sink failures are contained;
  previous/current state is retained. Fatal runtime failures are not deliberately caught.
  No experimental EmitLog API, reflection, or library-owned background thread is used.
end note
@enduml
